    fix(ralph-loop): bootstrap the state schema on a fresh database (QA-SPECLANG-5-R3)
    
    Root cause proved: `LoopController.create()` opened the state DB with the
    guarded opener and nothing else. better-sqlite3 creates the file and defers
    every schema question to the first statement, so the loop ran against a 0-byte
    database and died on its first query:
    
      $ cd <scratch> && npx tsx src/ralph-loop.ts <scratch>/speclang.db
      Ralph Loop failed: SqliteError: no such table: tasks
          at LoopController.getNextTask (src/ralph-loop.ts:69)
      exit=1            (0-byte speclang.db left on disk)
    
    Two facts found while proving it, both load-bearing:
    
      * The default path is worse than the row: a fresh project has no `.speclang/`
        at all, and the run died before any table question with
        `TypeError: Cannot open database because the directory does not exist`.
      * Reusing the canonical initialiser ALONE would NOT have fixed the row. That
        initialiser (`migrate`, i.e. what `createDatabase()` runs) creates
        `ralph_tasks` — a different table from the `tasks` the loop queries — and a
        `steering_packets` whose JSON field is `content`, not the `payload` the loop
        writes. This module's own `schema.sql` was executed by nothing in the repo,
        and the loop's `loadSpecs()` filters `specs.layer`, a column the canonical
        `specs` table does not carry.
    
    Fix, all idempotent, on the fresh path only:
    
      * create the directory holding the DB when it is missing (fresh-project
        default path), skipping `:memory:` / `file:` URLs;
      * run the canonical state initialiser `migrate()` — reused, not copied, so
        there is no second copy of the state schema;
      * apply this module's own task schema (RALPH_LOOP_SCHEMA_SQL) and two additive
        column guards in the ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN style migrations.ts already uses
        for tables that predate a file: `steering_packets.payload` and `specs.layer`;
      * report any bootstrap failure as RalphLoopStateInitError (code
        RALPH_LOOP_INIT_FAILED) naming the file and the recovery step; a corrupt
        file still reports the shared CorruptStateDatabaseError diagnostic unchanged.
    
    After (verified in this worktree):
    
      $ cd <scratch dir, no .speclang/> && npx tsx src/ralph-loop.ts
      Ralph Loop state database ready at .speclang/speclang.db (schema version 8)
      exit=124 (loop still running when the 12s timeout stopped it)
      tables present: tasks,specs,steering_packets,schema_migrations,ralph_tasks
      tasks columns: id,title,description,status,assigned_to,created_at,updated_at
    
    The DDL is compiled in rather than read from src/ralph-loop/schema.sql because
    the published tarball ships dist/ only (the constraint src/db/migrations.ts
    documents). tests/db/ralph-loop-fresh-state.test.ts pins the copy to that file by
    applying schema.sql to its own database and diffing the resulting `tasks` table,
    so the two cannot drift silently.
    
    Residuals, deliberately not fixed here (each is a design decision, not a
    bootstrap fix):
    
      * src/ralph-loop/controller.ts (the module-dir LoopController) has the same
        missing-bootstrap defect, but its spec dir ships stale compiled .js beside
        the .ts and `index.ts` resolves './controller' to that stale .js, so a fix
        to its TypeScript alone would be unprovable — it needs a regeneration pass.
      * canonical `steering_packets.task_id` foreign-keys to `ralph_tasks`, while the
        loop writes ids from its own `tasks` table: the failure path
        (handleFailure) still cannot insert until the two ralph task models are
        reconciled. The column itself now exists, so the statement prepares.
      * migrate() logs "Applying migration N: ..." to stdout on first bootstrap, the
        same as SpecLangDB.initialize() — library callers of LoopController.create()
        see those lines once per fresh DB.
    
    Gates run in this worktree: npx tsc --noEmit (0, though tsconfig excludes
    src/ralph-loop.ts, so the edited file was also typechecked directly —
    tsc --ignoreConfig --noEmit ... specs/implementation.spec.dir/src/ralph-loop.ts
    = 0); npx vitest run tests/db/ralph-loop-fresh-state.test.ts
    tests/db/corrupt-state-db-surfaces.test.ts tests/db/corrupt-state-db.test.ts
    tests/db/migrations.test.ts = 4 files, 27 tests passed. The new test file was
    RED-proved by restoring the pre-fix file: 6 of 7 fail, quoting
    `SqliteError: no such table: tasks` and `TypeError: Cannot open database
    because the directory does not exist` verbatim.
    
    Committed with --no-verify as a NAMED exception authorised by this row's brief:
    the repo pre-commit hook runs a multi-minute guard and the foreman runs the full
    gate on the merged tree. No guard or judge verdict is claimed here — this commit
    is unaudited by that chain.
    
